values ($c$assisted-dying-uk-debate$c$, $c$Assisted Dying in the UK: Where the Debate Stands$c$, $c$article$c$::interview.reading_source_type, $c$Summary drawn from House of Commons Library and parliamentary records$c$, $c$https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/terminally-ill-adults-end-of-life-bills/$c$, $c$Status as of September 2026 — check for later developments.$c$, $c$Assisting suicide remains a criminal offence in England and Wales. A private member's bill to allow terminally ill adults with capacity, expected to die within six months, to request help to end their lives passed the House of Commons in June 2025 but fell when it did not complete its stages in the House of Lords before the end of the parliamentary session. A near-identical bill was introduced in 2026 and was defeated at its Commons second reading in September 2026 by 286 votes to 270. In Scotland, a separate bill fell at its final vote in March 2026. Supporters argue for autonomy and relief of suffering, pointing to jurisdictions such as Oregon and Australian states with safeguards. Opponents raise concerns about pressure on vulnerable and disabled people, the adequacy of palliative care, safeguards, and the role of doctors. Professional bodies have moved to more neutral positions over time, but views among doctors remain divided.$c$, $c$One of the most likely ethics debate questions. A current, accurate picture — and fair presentation of both sides — shows maturity.$c$, $c$["Assisted dying is illegal in England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland as of September 2026.","The 2024–26 bill passed the Commons but fell in the Lords; the 2026 bill was defeated at second reading.","Key arguments: autonomy and suffering versus protection of the vulnerable and the state of palliative care.","Distinguish assisted dying from withdrawing treatment and from palliative care, which are lawful."]$c$::jsonb, $c$Use the Balanced Argument framework; state facts neutrally, steelman both sides, and conclude respectfully without claiming certainty.$c$, 4, $c$2026-09-29$c$, 'published')
