values ($c$when-breath-becomes-air$c$, $c$When Breath Becomes Air$c$, $c$book$c$::interview.reading_source_type, $c$Paul Kalanithi$c$, null, $c$Published posthumously in 2016.$c$, $c$Paul Kalanithi was a neurosurgical resident nearing the end of his training when he was diagnosed with advanced lung cancer in his mid-thirties. His memoir traces his path from studying literature and philosophy to medicine, driven by questions about what makes life meaningful, and then his experience of becoming a patient. He describes how illness changed his relationship with his oncologist, how he wrestled with uncertainty about how much time he had, and how he and his wife decided to have a child. The book was completed after his death, with an epilogue by his wife. It is a meditation on meaning, identity, and how doctors and patients face mortality — and on the value of being told the truth with compassion rather than numbers alone.$c$, $c$Offers the rare perspective of a doctor becoming a patient — powerful for questions on empathy, communicating uncertainty and why medicine matters.$c$, $c$["Illness challenges identity and meaning, not just the body.","Patients may want understanding more than statistics.","Doctors are also vulnerable; empathy runs both ways."]$c$::jsonb, $c$Use when discussing breaking bad news, empathy or motivation — e.g. how prognosis should be communicated.$c$, 2, $c$2026-09-29$c$, 'published')
